      "spam": false,
      "value": "This suggestion is good, but I'd like to revise it with slightly more effective/relevant limits:\n\n- Polycount has almost nothing to do with performance. A high-poly avatar (say, 19,999 tris) has essentially the same performance hit as a low-poly (say, 4 tris) avatar at this point in time. Polycount hits GPU utilization, and VRChat is 99.9% CPU-bound. However, maybe this \"performance-friendly\" mode could be configurable?\n- Skinned mesh renderer: Agreed.\n- Standard shaders only: Again, this is mostly a GPU-boundedness thing. On computers using very old integrated GPUs, you might have issues caused by shaders, but this should not be the target system for VRChat. Shaders, compared to other performance hogs, are minimal cost.\n- No animation overrides: To be honest, this is mostly irrelevant to performance.\n- Animations: See above\n- EDIT: Particles: See above. See my next comment for elaboration.\n- Cloth physics: Agreed, probably. On some systems, cloth physics is handled by CPU, some handle it on GPU if the PhysX API is present. \n- Dynamic bones: Agreed.\n- EDIT- Lights. 100% agreed.\n\nHowever, the base issue isn't necessarily the limits of this proposed system-- its the existence of it as a whole. VRChat lists minimum requirements of a GTX 970 and a 4000-series Intel CPU. If you go below that, performance cannot be guaranteed. Attempting to support hardware lower than the minimum requirements kind of invalidates the point of having minimum requirements. If VRChat wants to lower their minimum system requirements, then these might be good goals to have. \n\nAdditionally, the cost of detecting avatars, enforcing the system, and maintaining it over future versions may outweigh the arguably minimal benefits. Even with this proposed system on, if you have 50 people in a room, you are going to have a low framerate. That is a fact of life until CPU-boundedness issues are solved.\n\nI will say that a lot of my revisions mostly have to do with \"this isn't what causes performance issues\" rather than \"do everything possible to increase performance.\" Additionally, I personally wouldn't be using this system if implemented, so...",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
