      "spam": false,
      "value": "@{58c62e995865713647a7115b|full_name}: Even if my performance suffers because of a large number of people in a room, I feel like essentially \"deleting\" someone's individuality via forced avatars (even if you could customize them) really detracts from immersion. On top of that, I wouldn't even have a choice in the matter if implemented as stated in the OP-- if an admin decides I have to be in a 10k poly avatar, I don't get a choice. I'm just forced into it. To be honest, if I was in a room where the system was enforced, I would probably just leave.\n\nHowever, speaking more technically on the proposed implementation parameters, a lot of these concerns are a results of a base misunderstanding of what causes performance issues in VRChat. For example:\n\nDue to the polylimit of 20k, many people incorrectly assume that polycount is the direct cause of performance issues. It is not. Polycount hits GPU usage, which is essentially never capped out in VRC (unless you are running a well-below-min-spec GPU). I've personally seen avatars/items in worlds with well over 1 million tris with zero hit to performance.\n\n\"Standard Shaders Only\" - Again, another GPU-load thing. You will not have issues with a substandard shader unless your GPU is well below min spec. I have only experienced shader-related lag with a very old Intel integrated GPU.\n\nAnimation overrides/animation - This is yet another GPU-load thing, for the most part. Rarely will an animation cause CPU issues unless you enable a TON of new meshes/materials with the animation. The common misconception is that particle systems are inherently \"laggy\"-- this isn't the case except with extremely high particle counts, or with particles that use (for example) refraction in the shader, resulting in layered refraction. \n\nFor a tl;dr: I feel as if this system as-proposed is built on misunderstanding of what causes performance issues. I'd rather we teach/soft-enforce good practice with optimization across the board via VRC/community-created tutorials and guides than force even-more-strict standards on players. Additionally, the community as a whole needs to educate/be educated on what the performance hurdles in VRC actually are, rather than rely on hearsay and rumor.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
